## Resource Name

Every font follows the given naming sequence `h%02x-C%02X.sqf`. Where the
first value is the pixel height of the font, and the second value is the
page of the font (codepoint divided by 256). This allows the ability to only
have to load a small subset of a font to draw glyphs which are in similar
positions.

Every valid font must have the first codepage always available. This would
be in the file `h%02x-C00.sqf`. This contains the fallback glyph for the
entire character set which is at index zero. It is also used to load
information on the font without needing to scan or guess which codepages are
actually valid.

## Resource Stream Structure

The font resource uses the given structure, note that types match the types
that Java utilizes. Generally, a valid font will have all tables contain the
same number of entries.

Note that _Bitmap scans_ refers to _Bitmap scan lengths_
as in the number of bytes each bitmap takes in a scanline and not the actual
bitmap data.

 * Font Header -- This is always at the start.
   * _0_: `int` -- Pixel height of the font.
   * _4_: `int` -- The family identifier of the font.
     * `0` -- Regular.
     * `1` -- Monospace.
     * `2` -- Sans Serif.
     * `3` -- Serif.
   * _8_: `int` -- The ascent of the font.
   * _12_: `int` -- The descent of the font.
   * _16_: `int` -- Bounding Box X Coordinate.
   * _20_: `int` -- Bounding Box Y Coordinate.
   * _24_: `int` -- Bounding Box Width.
   * _28_: `int` -- Bounding Box Height.
   * _32_: `int` -- The codepoint at which the font starts at.
   * _36_: `int` -- The number of codepoints within the font.
   * _40_: `int` -- The size of the huffman bit table, in bytes.
   * _44_: `int` -- The size of the character bitmap table, in bytes.
 * Glyph Widths -- The width of every glyph.
   * _0_: `short n` -- The number of entries in the table.
   * _2_: `struct (1)[n]` -- The character width table. 
     * `unsigned byte width` -- Character width.
 * Glyph X Offsets -- The X offsets of every glyph.
   * _0_: `short n` -- The number of entries in the table.
   * _2_: `struct (1)[n]` -- The X offset table.
     * `byte xOffset` -- Character bitmap X offset.
 * Glyph Y Offsets -- The Y offsets of every glyph.
   * _0_: `short n` -- The number of entries in the table.
   * _2_: `struct (1)[n]` -- The Y offset table.
     * `byte yOffset` -- Character bitmap Y offset.
 * Glyph Flags -- The flags for each glyph.
   * _0_: `short n` -- The number of entries in the table.
   * _2_: `struct (1)[n]` -- The glyph flag table.
     * `byte flag` -- The flags for each glyph.
       * `0x01` -- Glyph is valid and is stored within the font. This is used
          as fonts store a single linear set of glyphs however not all glyphs
          may be in the font.
       * `0x02` -- Compression is used for this glyph.
       * `0x04` -- Reserved.
       * `0x08` -- Reserved.
       * `0x10` -- Reserved.
       * `0x20` -- Reserved.
       * `0x40` -- Reserved.
       * `0x80` -- Reserved.
 * Glyph Bitmap Offsets -- Offsets to the individual glyph bitmap data.
   * _0_: `short n` -- The number of entries in the table.
   * _2_: `struct (2)[n]` -- The offsets to the glyph data.
     * `unsigned short offset` -- The offset within the character font
        bitmap data where the bitmap of this font is stored. This chunk of
        data is later in the file, and thus this offset does not refer to
        the current position or the start of file.
 * Glyph Bitmap Scan Lengths -- The length of each bitmap scanline.
   * _0_: `short n` -- The number of entries in the table.
   * _2_: `struct (1)[n]` -- The bitmap scan length table.
     * `byte scanLen` -- The scanline length for the given bitmap. If a
        bitmap is 8 pixels then this will be 1 byte, if it is 9 pixels it
        will be 2 bytes. The formula is `1 + (width / 8)`.
 * Glyph Bitmap Data -- Raw glyph bitmap data.
   * _0_: `short size` -- The length of this table in bytes.
   * _2_: `byte[size]` -- Raw bitmap data.
 * Glyph Huffman Bits -- Huffman bit data for RaFoCES compressed fonts.
   * _0_: `short size` -- The length of this table in bytes.
   * _2_: `byte[size]` -- Raw huffman bit data.

## Bitmap Encoding Order

As an example, the replacement glyph has a bitmap scan length of two, thus
every byte represents 16 pixels. The replacement glyph is a clear question
mark within a diamond. The position of the question mark is marked
with hyphens (`-`) instead of periods (`.`) to indicate its layout and
position. From this, the least significant bit is the first/zeroth pixel,
whereas the most significant bit is the last/seventh pixel.

As this bitmap has a height of 12 pixels, the bitmap thus has a height of
12 pixels. This is the case for all bitmaps.

The representation on the right is how it would be rendered on screen, note
that the scans are in least-significant order.

```
0b12345678 | 0b12345678 -> 1234567890123456
-----------|-----------    ----------------
0b...#.... | 0b........ -> ....#...........
0b..###... | 0b........ -> ...###..........
0b.##-##.. | 0b........ -> ..##-##.........
0b##-#-##. | 0b........ -> .##-#-##........
0b##-##### | 0b.......# -> #####-###.......
0b###-###. | 0b........ -> .###-###........
0b.#####.. | 0b........ -> ..#####.........
0b..#-#... | 0b........ -> ...#-#..........
0b...#.... | 0b........ -> ....#...........
0b........ | 0b........ -> ................
0b........ | 0b........ -> ................
0b........ | 0b........ -> ................
```

## Built-in C Structure

This is a standard C structure that the conversion tool outputs, it is mostly
equivalent to the resource stream format and is here as a reference only as
this is a copy-paste from SquirrelJME's header file.

```c++
/**
 * Flags for a specific character.
 * 
 * @since 2024/06/04
 */
typedef enum sjme_scritchui_sqfFlag
{
	/** Character is valid. */
	SJME_SCRITCHUI_SQF_FLAG_VALID = 1,
	
	/** Compressed with RaFoCES w/ huffman table. */
	SJME_SCRITCHUI_SQF_FLAG_RAFOCES = 2,
} sjme_scritchui_sqfFlag;

/**
 * Represents the SQF family.
 * 
 * @since 2024/06/14
 */
typedef enum sjme_scritchui_sqfFamily
{
	/** Regular font. */
	SJME_SCRITCHUI_SQF_FAMILY_REGULAR,
	
	/** Monospace. */
	SJME_SCRITCHUI_SQF_FAMILY_MONOSPACE,
	
	/** Sans Serif. */
	SJME_SCRITCHUI_SQF_FAMILY_SANS_SERIF,
	
	/** Serif. */
	SJME_SCRITCHUI_SQF_FAMILY_SERIF,
	
	/** The number of families. */
	SJME_SCRITCHUI_NUM_SQF_FAMILY
} sjme_scritchui_sqfFamily;

/**
 * SQF Font information.
 *
 * @since 2019/06/20
 */
typedef struct sjme_scritchui_sqf
{
	/** The name of the font. */
	sjme_lpcstr name;
	
	/** The font family. */
	sjme_jint family;
	
	/** The pixel height of the font. */
	sjme_jint pixelHeight;
	
	/** The ascent of the font. */
	sjme_jint ascent;
	
	/** The descent of the font. */
	sjme_jint descent;
	
	/** Bounding box X offset. */
	sjme_jint bbx;
	
	/** Bounding box Y offset. */
	sjme_jint bby;
	
	/** Bounding box width. */
	sjme_jint bbw;
	
	/** Bounding box height. */
	sjme_jint bbh;
	
	/** The starting codepoint for this font. */
	sjme_jint codepointStart;
	
	/** The number of codepoints which are in this font. */
	sjme_jint codepointCount;
	
	/** The size of the @c huffBits member. */
	sjme_jint huffBitsSize;
	
	/** The size of the @c charBmp member. */
	sjme_jint charBmpSize;
	
	/** Huffman bits for huffman tables. */
	const sjme_jbyte* huffBits;
	
	/** Widths for each character. */
	const sjme_jbyte* charWidths;
	
	/** X offset for character. */
	const sjme_jbyte* charXOffset;
	
	/** Y offset for character. */
	const sjme_jbyte* charYOffset;
	
	/** SQF Font Flags, per character. */
	const sjme_jbyte* charFlags;
	
	/** Offset to the character bitmap for the given character. */
	const sjme_jshort* charBmpOffset;
	
	/** The bytes per scanline for each character. */
	const sjme_jbyte* charBmpScan;
	
	/** Which characters make up the bitmap? */
	const sjme_jbyte* charBmp;
} sjme_scritchui_sqf;

typedef struct sjme_scritchui_sqfCodepage
{
	/** The name of the font. */
	sjme_lpcstr name;
	
	/** The number of codepages in the font. */
	sjme_jint numCodepages;
	
	/** The codepages for the font. */
	const sjme_scritchui_sqf* const* codepages;
} sjme_scritchui_sqfCodepage;
```

## Creation and Conversion

Generally SquirrelJME fonts are authored in _FontForge_ and are stored
within the repository with the _.sfdir_ format. This means that every glyph
is its own individual file, this makes it simpler to change a single glyph
and have version control for it rather than as a bulk font. This is a text
based format.

Glyph Bitmap Distribution Format (`BDF`) is also supported as a basis for
font data, this is an older format that is generally used by X11. This is
a text based format.

Conversion is done via the `squirreljme-font` tool. It handles both `BDF`
and _.sfdir_ fonts.

It can output to a C struct format that is used for built-in SquirrelJME fonts
that are intended to be used as an emergency fallback in the event that there
are no system fonts available in any fashion.

The tool is invoked with the following:

 * Usage: `[in.bdf|in.strike] [out.zip|-|out.c|-.c] --compress`

## Future Thoughts/Ideas

These are future thoughts and ideas for the format.

### Static Compression Vector Tables

Similar to how Deflate has fixed huffman codes for very common sequences, it
would be very optimal to also have fixed codes for the most common sequences
of vector directions. This is so that each font does not have to store the
full dynamic table for the glyph.

### Entire Font Huffman Table

Rather than keeping the huffman table per group of glyphs, it could instead
be combined into a single huffman chunk reference. This would be required for
any glyph to be loaded, however it would greatly reduce the amount of memory
needed as now there is just a single huffman table for every single glyph.
